Usage: nettput -c <host> [options]

Measure bulk TCP throughput and CPU cost per byte against
graviton/scripts/nettput-peer.py running on <host>.

Usage: disktput -f <path> [options]

Measure disk throughput, IOPS, latency and CPU cost per byte, either
on a raw device (/dev/disk/nvme/0/raw) or through the file system.

  -f <path>     device or file to measure (required)
  -m <mode>     seqread | seqwrite | randread | randwrite | verify
                (default seqread)
  -b <bytes>    block size per operation, K/M suffixes ok (default 64K)
  -n <bytes>    total bytes to move across all threads (default 512M)
  -t <n>        concurrent threads, which is the request depth the
                device sees (default 1, max 256)
  -s <bytes>    span to operate within (default: -n, or the whole
                device). Make this much larger than RAM for random
                reads, or the page cache answers instead of the disk
  -o <bytes>    offset the span starts at (default 0)
  -D            open with O_NOCACHE, bypassing the file cache
  -S            fsync once at the end and report its cost separately
  -F            fsync after every write (durability, not throughput)
  -e <n>        random seed, so a random run is repeatable (default 1)
  -T <secs>     run for this long instead of for -n bytes. Use this for
                any concurrency sweep: a fixed -n makes high thread
                counts finish too fast for the EBS token bucket to
                bind, and throughput then appears to exceed the
                instance's own documented ceiling
  -A <bytes>    I/O buffer alignment, power of two (default one page).
                Below a page, nvme_disk bounces the request through a
                16 KiB buffer, so this is not a cosmetic knob
  -U <bytes>    deliberately offset the buffer this far past its
                alignment, to measure the bounce path on purpose
  -i <secs>     trace progress every <secs>, so a path that stalls can
                be told apart from one that is merely slow, and a
                wedge from either
  -P            stamp every written block with its own offset, so a
                later -m verify can prove the data is the data that
                belongs there -- not zeros, and not some other
                block. Use for durability tests across a stop/start
  -y            confirm writing to a raw device, destroying its contents
  -J            also print one tab-separated line for a harness
  -L <label>    label echoed into the result
